<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<handout>
<title>Identifying First-Order Differential Equations</title>

<introduction>

<p>
Visit each station and work with your group to answer the questions.
Do not focus on completing the entire solution. Instead, focus on
identifying the type of differential equation and determining the next step
in the solution process.
</p>

<p>
At each station:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Identify the type of differential equation.</li>
<li>Explain how you recognized it.</li>
<li>Determine the next step in the solution process.</li>
<li>Record your reasoning.</li>
</ol>

</introduction>

<!-- Station 1 -->

<exercise workspace="2in">

<p>
Consider the differential equation
</p>

<me>
\frac{dy}{dx}=xy+x.
</me>

<p>
Hints:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Can you separate the variables?</li>
<li>Can all <m>x</m>-terms be placed on one side and all <m>y</m>-terms on the other?</li>
</ul>

<p>
Complete the following:
</p>

<ol>
<li>What type of differential equation is this?</li>
<li>How do you know?</li>
<li>What would be your next step?</li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<workspace/>

<!-- Station 2 -->

<exercise workspace="2in">


<p>
Consider the differential equation
</p>

<me>
y'+3y=6.
</me>

<p>
Hints:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Does the equation match the form <m>y'+p(x)y=g(x)</m>?</li>
<li>If so, what is the integrating factor?</li>
</ul>

<p>
Complete the following:
</p>

<ol>
<li>What type of differential equation is this?</li>
<li>How do you know?</li>
<li>What would be your next step?</li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<workspace/>

<!-- Station 3 -->

<exercise workspace="2in">


<p>
Consider the differential equation
</p>

<me>
(2xy+4)\,dx+(x^2+3)\,dy=0.
</me>

<p>
Hints:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Identify <m>M(x,y)</m> and <m>N(x,y)</m>.</li>
<li>Compute <m>\frac{\partial M}{\partial y}</m>.</li>
<li>Compute <m>\frac{\partial N}{\partial x}</m>.</li>
<li>Compare the two derivatives.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Complete the following:
</p>

<ol>
<li>What type of differential equation is this?</li>
<li>How do you know?</li>
<li>What would be your next step?</li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<workspace/>

<!-- Station 4 -->

<exercise workspace="2in">

<p>
Consider the differential equation
</p>

<me>
y'+2y=xy^3.
</me>

<p>
Hints:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Does the equation fit the form <m>y'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n</m>?</li>
<li>What is the value of <m>n</m>?</li>
<li>What substitution is commonly used?</li>
</ul>

<p>
Complete the following:
</p>

<ol>
<li>What type of differential equation is this?</li>
<li>How do you know?</li>
<li>What would be your next step?</li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<workspace/>

<!-- Station 5 -->

<exercise workspace="2in">

<p>
The temperature of freshly poured concrete on a bridge deck is
<m>80^\circ\text{C}</m>.
The surrounding air temperature is <m>20^\circ\text{C}</m>.
Engineers assume the cooling rate is proportional to the difference
between the concrete temperature and the air temperature.
</p>

<p>
Hints:
</p>

<ul>
<li>What quantity is changing?</li>
<li>What quantity determines the rate of change?</li>
<li>What does the phrase “proportional to” suggest?</li>
</ul>

<p>
Complete the following:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Define a variable for temperature.</li>
<li>Write a differential equation that models the situation.</li>
<li>What type of differential equation is it?</li>
<li>What solution method would you use?</li>
</ol>

</exercise>


</handout>
